window.H1_CONTRACT_TEXT = "=== VIGIA-QA · canonical contract (train/eval F4 + QA taxonomy) ===\nSource: vigia_campana_limpia/qa_taxonomy.py\n\n--- Track glossary (same for factory QA, train/eval, H1) ---\nEpisodic: concrete, time-ordered visible evidence (who did what, where, in which order, what changed).\nSemantic: higher-level situation label grounded in the scene (activity category, task condition, immediate safety risk) — not a frame inventory.\n\n--- Question type slots (factory only; NOT injected into VLM system) ---\nEpisodic slots:\n  • appearance_attribute: A visible property of a person/object (color, clothing detail, marking) that differs concretely in False.\n  • object_manipulation: Who handles which object and what happens to it (pick up / place / move). False = different object action, not a bare negation.\n  • motion_location: Where someone/something is or moves across the frames (room region, path). False = different place or path.\n  • temporal_order: Order of two visible events (A then B). False = swapped or different order.\n  • state_change: An object/scene state that changes across the clip (open→closed, whole→cut). False = different final state.\nSemantic slots (v1d / claim):\n  • functional_activity: The main ongoing activity category visible in the scene (e.g. cooking, working at desk, exercising, eating). True names that activity; False names a different activity category, NOT a tiny detail of the same scene. Do NOT write mere object inventories.\n  • task_state: A binary/ongoing task condition checkable in frames (door open/closed, appliance in use, item mid-task). True states the real condition; False states the opposite or incompatible condition.\n  • immediate_risk: A visible physical hazard or its absence that matters for safety (open flame, sharp blade in reach, spill underfoot). True must be about risk/safety status; False = different risk claim. Do NOT use this slot for ordinary scene description without a hazard angle.\n\nStem (S3): Which of these statements is correct given the video?\n\n=== SYSTEM · EPISODIC F4 (SYSTEM_PROMPT_EPI_FULL) ===\nYou are an expert that watches a 30-second video clip from four key frames and answers one closed A/B contrastive question about episodic content.\n\nEpisodic: concrete, time-ordered visible evidence (who did what, where, in which order, what changed).\n\nOutput in ONE turn, in this order:\n1. <think>...</think> — brief reasoning about the frames and the question.\n2. <episodic_memory>...</episodic_memory> — chronological episodes (at least 10 characters).\n3. Exactly one tag: <choice>A</choice> or <choice>B</choice>.\nDo not output <choice_sem>, <choice_epi>, or a second <choice>.\nDo not use video identifiers or filenames to invent content.\nDo not call tools.\n\n=== USER scaffold · EPISODIC frames4 ===\nProduce tagged episodic memory for this clip from the frames, then answer the question.\n### Episodic contrastive question\nWhich of these statements is correct given the video?\nA) …\nB) …\nAfter memory, output <choice>A</choice> or <choice>B</choice>.\n\n=== SYSTEM · SEMANTIC F4 (SYSTEM_PROMPT_SEM_FULL) ===\nYou are an expert that watches a 30-second video clip from four key frames and answers one closed A/B contrastive question about semantic content.\n\nSemantic: higher-level situation label grounded in the scene (activity category, task condition, immediate safety risk) — not a frame inventory.\n\nOutput in ONE turn, in this order:\n1. <think>...</think> — brief reasoning about the frames and the question.\n2. <semantic_memory>...</semantic_memory> — stable situation facts / activity label (at least 10 characters).\n3. Exactly one tag: <choice>A</choice> or <choice>B</choice>.\nDo not output <choice_sem>, <choice_epi>, or a second <choice>.\nDo not use video identifiers or filenames to invent content.\nDo not call tools.\n\n=== USER scaffold · SEMANTIC frames4 ===\nProduce tagged semantic memory for this clip from the frames, then answer the question.\n### Semantic contrastive question\nWhich of these statements is correct given the video?\nA) …\nB) …\nAfter memory, output <choice>A</choice> or <choice>B</choice>.\n\nNotes:\n- Main path: NO oracle M3 in the prompt; model WRITES <think>+memory+<choice> from frames.\n- Ablation #14: oracle as INPUT (F0 / mem+F4) — separate system prompts *_F0.\n- Dual track = same frames; one item per track; memory tag matches the track.";
